+## Subtopics
 
-**Edge Cases:**
-*   **Geometric Ambiguity:** AdaNeRF improves quality in geometrically ambiguous regions compared to methods requiring depth maps, as it does not rely on explicit depth estimation [S1].
-*   **Initialization:** Requires a pre-trained NeRF to initialize the color network for best results, as end-to-end training from scratch may be unstable [S1].
+### Sampling Networks
+Methods that use a network to predict sample locations or weights, reducing the need for dense uniform sampling. AdaNeRF uses this to predict weights for fixed positions [S1].
+
+### Shading Networks
+The component that computes density and color for selected samples. AdaNeRF uses a heavier network only for significant samples [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Reduced compared to standard NeRF because the expensive Shading Network is evaluated only $K$ times per ray instead of $N$ times. The lightweight Sampling Network is evaluated once per ray.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Similar to NeRF for the Shading Network, but potentially lower if the Sampling Network is very small.
-*   **Performance:** Enables real-time rendering after fine-tuning [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Adaptable to meet performance targets; reduces sampling cost significantly [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires a two-stage training process (pre-training NeRF, then fine-tuning with Sampling Network) [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Reduced compared to standard NeRF due to fewer shading network evaluations per ray. The sampling network is lightweight and evaluated once per ray [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Compact neural representation after fine-tuning [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Enables real-time rendering after fine-tuning to the desired number of samples [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Adaptable to meet performance targets for real-time applications; improves quality in geometrically ambiguous regions by not requiring a depth map [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires a pre-trained NeRF for initialization to achieve best results [S1].
 
 ## Background
-AdaNeRF was introduced by Kurz et al. [KNL* 22] as part of the broader effort to accelerate neural radiance fields. It is cited in recent surveys on 3D representations [S1] and tutorials on NeRF extensions [S2, unofficial]. The method falls under the category of "baking neural fields" or "adaptive sampling" techniques that reduce the number of sample points [S2, unofficial].
+AdaNeRF was introduced by Kurz et al. [KNL* 22] in 2022. It is part of the broader effort to accelerate neural radiance fields by reducing the number of sample points [S2, unofficial]. The method builds on the idea of using sampling networks to localize surfaces or predict importance, as seen in TermiNeRF [PC21], but adapts it for real-time rendering by using fixed sample positions and incremental sparsity [S1].
 
